What does this code print, and why?

```csharp
int first = 6;
int second = first;
first = 11;
second = second + 2;
Console.WriteLine($"{first}, {second}");
```

What does this code print? Explain how copying each argument leads to that result.

```csharp
int number = 3;
int[] items = { 4 };

Change(number, items);
Console.WriteLine($"{number}, {items[0]}");

static void Change(int number, int[] items)
{
    number = 7;
    items[0] = 8;
    items = new[] { 99 };
    items[0] = 100;
}
```

Consider this C# code:

```csharp
static void Bump(ref int value)
{
    value += 2;
}

static void Produce(out int value)
{
    value = 9;
}

int a = 4;
int b;
Bump(ref a);
Produce(out b);
```

What are the final values of `a` and `b`, and why do the assignments reach those variables? Explain why `a` must be initialized before its call while `b` need not be, and what `Produce` must do before returning normally.

Trace this code. What does it print, what storage does `value` refer to in each call, and would uncommenting the assignment compile? Explain how the parameter rules determine your answer.

```csharp
static void Show(in int value)
{
    // value = 20;
    Console.WriteLine(value);
}

int number = 6;
Show(in number);
Show(number + 3);
Console.WriteLine(number);
```

